const SPORT={
  key:'sbk',name:'WorldSBK',brandTag:'An ode to World Superbike · 1988–2026',title:'APEX / An Ode to World Superbike · 1988–2026',
  description:'Thirty-nine seasons of World Superbike drawn as a river of manufacturer colours, every circuit drawn to its own line, every race replayed grid to flag. 1988–2026.',
  gapLabel:'Interval',
  raceNoun:'races',raceNounOne:'race',raceShort:'race',sprintName:'Superpole Race',
  isMain:t=>t==='R1'||t==='R2',sprintAsRace:true,
  formatLabel:t=>t==='SPR'?'Superpole Race':t==='R1'?'Race 1':t==='R2'?'Race 2':t,
  raceName:r=>r.name,
  riverLede:'Every championship point ever scored, flowing left to right in the colours of the manufacturer whose rider scored it — from the published riders’ standings of each season. The river widens as the calendar grows. <b>Hover a year</b> to read it; <b>click</b> to step onto its grid.',
  gridLede:n=>`${n} circuits have hosted World Superbike in this lens. Outlines are drawn from OpenStreetMap where the circuit is mapped there, or from the F1DB survey of the same venue for circuits both series have raced; a circuit with neither shows its history as a ring of winners. Cards are lit in the colours of the most successful manufacturer there and sized by how often the calendar returned — two or three races a weekend.`,
  colours:{'ROC-Yamaha':'#7d9bff','Petronas':'#3ec6b8','EBR':'#ff9e80','Benelli':'#8fd18f','Unresolved make':'#5b616b','Ducati':'#f34048','Kawasaki':'#7ae02a','Yamaha':'#497bff','Honda':'#f44353','BMW':'#66b5ff','Bimota':'#e8edf4','Aprilia':'#ec565d','Suzuki':'#39a0f4','Petronas':'#29c7ba','MV Agusta':'#cbb888','EBR':'#f5ae46','Benelli':'#8fd68a'},
  eras:[
    {id:'first',short:'Beginnings',from:1988,to:1999,name:'The beginnings',text:'Merkel, Polen, Russell, Fogarty and Kocinski: the championship takes shape around production-based twins and fours.',bike:{label:'First-generation superbike',features:['Production-based','Twin headlight fairing','Under-engine exhaust'],wire:false,fairing:true,tail:false,exhaust:'twin',headlight:true}},
    {id:'2000',short:'2000s',from:2000,to:2009,name:'Turn of the century',text:'Edwards, Bayliss, Toseland and Spies; a new generation of champions and the 1000cc four-cylinder rules.',bike:{label:'2000s superbike',features:['1000cc fours join the twins','Underseat exhaust','Road-derived chassis'],wire:false,fairing:true,tail:true,exhaust:'under',headlight:true}},
    {id:'2010',short:'2010s',from:2010,to:2018,name:'The 2010s',text:'Biaggi, Sykes, Guintoli and the rise of Rea: Kawasaki’s long green run.',bike:{label:'2010s superbike',features:['Electronics packages','Side exhaust','Compact tail'],wire:false,fairing:true,tail:true,exhaust:'single',headlight:true}},
    {id:'three',short:'Three races',from:2019,to:2026,name:'Three-race weekends',text:'Race 1, the Superpole Race and Race 2 every weekend. All-race records here include Superpole Races, as the championship counts them.',bike:{label:'Modern superbike with wings',features:['Superpole Race weekends','Aerodynamic wings','Production V4s and fours'],wire:false,fairing:true,tail:true,exhaust:'single',wings:true,headlight:true}}],
  sourcesHtml:`<h3>The archive, explained</h3><p class="small muted">World Superbike Championship only, from its first season in 1988. WorldSSP, WorldSSP300, WorldSPB, WorldWCR and national Superbike series are outside this edition. Rebuilt in September 2026 entirely from openly licensed sources; nothing from the series’ own results service or artwork is carried.</p><ol><li><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/">Wikipedia</a> season articles (<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, Wikipedia contributors)<p>Every result, calendar and published championship standing is transcribed from the season articles’ calendars and result matrices; each race and each season’s standings link to the article they came from, and the archive records the revision and retrieval date of every page. Blank cells stay blank: no lap time, gap, lap count or unpublished position is inferred. Snapshot __SNAPSHOT__.</p></li><li><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://www.wikidata.org/">Wikidata</a> (CC0 1.0)<p>Circuit coordinates and countries.</p></li><li><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap</a> (© OpenStreetMap contributors, <a href="https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ODbL</a>)<p>Circuit outlines are the mapped raceway geometry around each venue’s coordinates — the current footprint, not a historical layout — where the venue is mapped. No imagery from the series’ own sites is used.</p></li><li><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://github.com/f1db/f1db">F1DB</a> (<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY 4.0</a>)<p>Where OpenStreetMap has no raceway for a venue but Formula 1 has raced there, the outline is the F1DB survey of that venue in the layout nearest the year of the race; motorcycle layouts can differ, and the circuit page says which source it is using. A circuit with neither shows its history as a ring of winners.</p></li></ol><h3>How to read the statistics</h3><p class="small muted">Latest included race: __LASTDATE__. 2026 is an incomplete season. Race 1, Race 2 and the Superpole Race are all counted as races, as the championship counts them; the format is shown on every race. Wins and podiums count published positions; recorded entries count rows in the result matrices. Pole positions and fastest laps are lower bounds wherever a matrix omits them. Championship points are not recorded per race: the river and the season views use each season’s published riders’ standings and manufacturers’ standings. The 1988 Donington race victories are counted individually; the combined-result points of that round are preserved in the published totals. The grid-to-flag replay starts bikes in finishing order where the grid is not recorded. This is a dated snapshot of freely licensed sources, not a live feed; the data files are offered under the same CC BY-SA 4.0 terms.</p>`
};

